  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 104 PM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nDry conditions will prevail Saturday as the aforementioned upper\nlow propagates toward the Baja Peninsula and ridging develops \nover the Southwest. High temperatures will remain below normal for\nthis time of year through the weekend. By Saturday evening into \nSunday, the upper low begins to weaken and may begin to lift to \nthe the northeast, allowing for some moisture to return. Overall \nforcing is minimal, so precipitation changes are on the lower \nside. However, the greatest chances will be focused over western \nNew Mexico where the low's eventual track and the greatest \nmoisture advection will likely set up. \n\nOnce we move into early next week, attention turns to a strong\nPacific low pressure system that will dig across the West Coast\nbefore traversing inland. Increasing moisture ahead of this system\nshould support a gradual return of precipitation chances early to\nmidweek. Forecast timing and placement of this system remains\nuncertain, but current guidance suggests Thursday/Friday could be\nthe most favorable day for more widespread precipitation. Details\nwill continue to be refined the closer we get to the event. Cooler\ntemperatures are still expected to persist, and snow will be\npossible across the highest peaks if temperatures and eventual\nsystem track remain favorable.",
